<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ I</p>
      <h2 id="four">The roster</h2>
    </div>
    <p>All declared in <code>.mcp.json</code> at the repository root, so the capability travels with the project.</p>
  </div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>Provides</th><th>Delivery</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="phase">context7</td><td>Current library documentation, so a third-party API is verified, never recalled</td><td>fetched on demand</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">plantuml</td><td>Diagram rendering and syntax validation for specs</td><td>fetched on demand</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">playwright</td><td>Browser automation for cross-engine visual checks</td><td>fetched on demand</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">sprint-channel</td><td>A file-locked coordination channel for multi-session work</td><td>first-party, ships bundled</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The roster splits in two. Three servers are required and checked by name. <code>context7</code> is the default satisfier of a documentation requirement, and the audit reports it when present but never fails when it is gone.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ II</p>
      <h2 id="outcome">Documentation is an outcome, not a tool</h2>
    </div>
    <p>The rule is that a third-party API must be verified against current documentation before code is written against it, and never recalled from training.</p>
  </div>
  <p>The rule names an outcome. It is satisfied by <code>context7</code>, by a library's own documentation, or by a pinned local cache, and a project may remove <code>context7</code> entirely as long as the outcome holds.</p>
  <p>Hard-mandating a service that prompts a login would have coupled every downstream user of an open-source baseline to a commercial signup, which is a dependency hiding inside a rule.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ III</p>
      <h2 id="channel">The channel</h2>
    </div>
    <p><code>sprint-channel</code> exposes nine tools, all declared in <code>.claude/mcp/sprint-channel/server.mjs</code>.</p>
  </div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="phase">register_peer</td><td>Register a peer, worker or session, on the channel</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">leave_peer</td><td>Deregister a peer and remove it from <code>peers[]</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">send_message</td><td>Send a mechanical-coordination message to one peer</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">broadcast</td><td>Send a coordination message to every peer</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">claim_task</td><td>Claim a task atomically, file-locked and race-safe</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">signal_done</td><td>Mark a task done and unblock its dependents</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">release_task</td><td>Lead re-dispatch: reset a claimed or yielded task to pending and resolve its open yield</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">raise_conflict</td><td>Raise a write-set conflict for lead arbitration</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">yield_fork</td><td>Yield an un-decidable fork to the lead</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><code>claim_task</code> takes its lock with <code>mkdirSync</code>, which is atomic on POSIX, so two peers racing for the same task produce one winner and one <code>EEXIST</code>. A lock older than the 30 second TTL counts as stale and is reclaimed by an atomic <code>renameSync</code>, so a crashed peer cannot leave a task unclaimable forever.</p>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
    <div class="body">
      <strong>The message types are a closed set.</strong>
      <p>That is a design decision rather than an oversight. There is no free-form directive type, so a design instruction cannot travel over the channel at all. Coordination goes through it; judgment does not.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ IV</p>
      <h2 id="bundling">Bundling</h2>
    </div>
    <p><code>sprint-channel</code> imports <code>@modelcontextprotocol/sdk</code> and <code>zod</code>. Both are devDependencies that never reach an install.</p>
  </div>
  <p><code>esbuild</code> inlines them at build time, so the shipped <code>server.mjs</code> is self-contained and the baseline carries no runtime dependency. The development tree keeps the readable source; only the shipped copy is bundled. That costs 1111 KB for <code>sprint-channel</code> and 563 KB for <code>sprint-pool</code>.</p>
  <p>Registering the server only became safe once that bundle existed. Before it, declaring the server would have handed consumers something that could not start.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ V</p>
      <h2 id="merge">Merge semantics on install and upgrade</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Servers named in the shipped template are baseline-canonical.</p>
  </div>
  <p>They are replaced wholesale from the template on every install and upgrade rather than merged key by key. A server under a name of your own is preserved exactly. A customisation applied to a baseline-named server is not, and that is the mechanism by which upgrades deliver changes to those servers rather than an accident.</p>
  <p>The guidance is one sentence: keep intentional customisations under a non-baseline name.</p>
  <p>Note the asymmetry. Inside the server map, the template wins. Outside it, your file wins, and template keys are only added when missing.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ VI</p>
      <h2 id="broker">The broker</h2>
    </div>
    <p><code>sprint-pool</code> is a second first-party server, bundled and deliberately unregistered.</p>
  </div>
  <p>It is a channel server rather than a stdio one, so declaring it in <code>.mcp.json</code> would ship a registration that cannot work. It stays inert until <code>.claude/skills/companion/launch.sh</code> activates it.</p>
  <p>Both servers sit on top of <code>sprint-broker</code>, which is transport only. It reuses <code>sprint-channel</code>'s handlers and adds a Unix-domain socket over <code>node:net</code> plus a frame codec, and that split is why it is not a server in its own right.</p>
  <p>One broker owns a channel. When a second broker hits <code>EADDRINUSE</code> it probes the socket before reclaiming it: a live broker answers and the newcomer refuses to start, while a crashed broker refuses the connection and leaves a socket that is safe to unlink. Silently taking over would split the pod and hand the new broker empty state.</p>
</section>
